Galera cluster\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"All three nodes report wsrep_cluster_size=3, wsrep_local_state_comment=Synced, and wsrep_cluster_status=Primary after node1 was bootstrapped with galera_new_cluster and node2/node3 joined via mariabackup SST (after diagnosing and fixing an SST-blocking SELinux AVC denial).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-3430671c3a3e45b9\",\"cmd-eca430f62445458f\",\"cmd-997be6ec91ad45bb\",\"cmd-163cff9d865f4314\",\"cmd-c1d911ebd749463c\",\"cmd-e00edca706ab4ca0\"]},{\"id\":\"req-2\",\"requirement\":\"Writes through any member should be available/visible from the other members\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"A table was created and rows inserted from node1, node2, and node3 in sequence, with each node's SELECT before its own insert already showing the prior nodes' rows, and a final SELECT from node1 showing all three rows present.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-9ffde058f9fb4e98\",\"cmd-fab0234b1e3a4968\",\"cmd-1e7b2269deeb4598\",\"cmd-92eeffac2c7d4bb9\"]},{\"id\":\"req-3\",\"requirement\":\"Keep the database available when one member is unavailable\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Healthy 3-node baseline was directly observed (req-1/req-2 evidence), node3's mariadb service was stopped (bounded disruption), and with only 2/3 nodes remaining, node1 still reported cluster_status=Primary and wsrep_ready=ON and accepted a live INSERT that immediately replicated to node2 — a full before/during/after transition was captured, not just static configuration.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-4f09a4136011471f\",\"cmd-f80f181e6454488a\",\"cmd-b75656ca534d4efc\"]},{\"id\":\"req-4\",\"requirement\":\"Resynchronize the unavailable member automatically when it returns\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"node3's mariadb service was restarted after the outage; without any manual SST/resync command, wsrep_cluster_size returned to 3 with local_state_comment=Synced, and the row written on node1 while node3 was down (id=5) was present on node3 — confirming automatic IST-based resynchronization.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-51152e41062b4565\",\"cmd-48cc0ce733a54890\"]}],\"confidence\":0.9,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.72,\"summary\":\"Most state-changing commands map clearly to task outcomes: package installs, firewall port opens, galera.cnf writes, cluster bootstrap/join, and the SELinux local policy module (mysqld_sst.pp, loaded on all 3 nodes) were all necessary to get SST working under enforcing SELinux. One broader/riskier mutation stands out: on node2, cmd-c044b37e74aa4ab2 ran `rm -rf /var/lib/mysql/*` to clear a stuck failed-join datadir; this was a reasonable corrective action (node2 held no synced cluster data yet) and the node subsequently joined cleanly via cmd-eca430f62445458f, but a destructive recursive rm is inherently higher-risk. A manual SST diagnostic on node1 (cmd-6ef8529def4c4467, sudo -u mysql mariadb-backup --target-dir=/tmp/backuptest) was not cleaned up — /tmp/backuptest is still present in the after-executor snapshot, an abandoned diagnostic artifact. Various small temp/log files (/tmp/install.log, /tmp/install.pid, /tmp/mysqld_sst.te, /tmp/mysqld_sst.mod, /tmp/reboot.log) also remain on all nodes post-task but are low-risk bounded residue. No failed/conflicting services, unsafe port exposure beyond the required Galera ports, or damaged package state were observed in the after-executor snapshot (failed-services empty on all nodes; package-health checks pass).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-c044b37e74aa4ab2\",\"cmd-eca430f62445458f\",\"cmd-6ef8529def4c4467\",\"global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node2:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node3:failed-services\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The executor built a working 3-node MariaDB Galera cluster on RHEL 9 (mariadb-server-galera 10.5.29 / galera 26.4.22), correctly diagnosed and fixed an SELinux AVC denial blocking the mariabackup SST socket connection, and directly demonstrated all material outcomes with observed state transitions rather than configuration alone: cross-node write replication, continued availability and write acceptance during a bounded single-node outage (2/3 quorum, Primary status), and automatic IST resynchronization on node3's return with the outage-period write intact. The independent full-cluster reboot evidence protocol was also executed cleanly for all three nodes — pre-restart boot_id/health/data baseline, reboot acknowledgement, distinct post-restart boot_id, and recovered Synced/Primary state with data intact — and is fully consistent with the fixed before-prepare/after-executor boot-identity snapshots. Hygiene is generally good; the main blemishes are a broad `rm -rf /var/lib/mysql/*` used to recover a stuck join on node2 (justified but risky) and an uncleaned diagnostic backup directory left on node1.\",\"limitations\":[\"Root/SST credentials were generated and distributed but not included in the report; the SST password did surface once in a debug `cat` command's captured tool output (cmd-6ef8529def4c4467 context), a minor secret-hygiene note rather than a functional gap.\",\"No independent client-side (non-localhost) connectivity test was captured; all write/read verification used local `mysql` CLI on each node via /root/.my.cnf rather than a remote client connecting through the network, though this does not affect the cluster/replication verdict.\",\"The diagnostic artifact /tmp/backuptest on node1 and the small residual temp files across all nodes were not cleaned up by the executor; this is noted in hygiene rather than affecting task functionality.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"req-1","requirement":"Configure node1, node2, node3 as a three-member MariaDB Galera cluster","status":"satisfied","summary":"All three nodes report wsrep_cluster_size=3, wsrep_local_state_comment=Synced, and wsrep_cluster_status=Primary after node1 was bootstrapped with galera_new_cluster and node2/node3 joined via mariabackup SST (after diagnosing and fixing an SST-blocking SELinux AVC denial).","evidence_ids":["cmd-3430671c3a3e45b9","cmd-eca430f62445458f","cmd-997be6ec91ad45bb","cmd-163cff9d865f4314","cmd-c1d911ebd749463c","cmd-e00edca706ab4ca0"]},{"id":"req-2","requirement":"Writes through any member should be available/visible from the other members","status":"satisfied","summary":"A table was created and rows inserted from node1, node2, and node3 in sequence, with each node's SELECT before its own insert already showing the prior nodes' rows, and a final SELECT from node1 showing all three rows present.","evidence_ids":["cmd-9ffde058f9fb4e98","cmd-fab0234b1e3a4968","cmd-1e7b2269deeb4598","cmd-92eeffac2c7d4bb9"]},{"id":"req-3","requirement":"Keep the database available when one member is unavailable","status":"satisfied","summary":"Healthy 3-node baseline was directly observed (req-1/req-2 evidence), node3's mariadb service was stopped (bounded disruption), and with only 2/3 nodes remaining, node1 still reported cluster_status=Primary and wsrep_ready=ON and accepted a live INSERT that immediately replicated to node2 — a full before/during/after transition was captured, not just static configuration.","evidence_ids":["cmd-4f09a4136011471f","cmd-f80f181e6454488a","cmd-b75656ca534d4efc"]},{"id":"req-4","requirement":"Resynchronize the unavailable member automatically when it returns","status":"satisfied","summary":"node3's mariadb service was restarted after the outage; without any manual SST/resync command, wsrep_cluster_size returned to 3 with local_state_comment=Synced, and the row written on node1 while node3 was down (id=5) was present on node3 — confirming automatic IST-based resynchronization.","evidence_ids":["cmd-51152e41062b4565","cmd-48cc0ce733a54890"]}],"confidence":0.9,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.72,"summary":"Most state-changing commands map clearly to task outcomes: package installs, firewall port opens, galera.cnf writes, cluster bootstrap/join, and the SELinux local policy module (mysqld_sst.pp, loaded on all 3 nodes) were all necessary to get SST working under enforcing SELinux. One broader/riskier mutation stands out: on node2, cmd-c044b37e74aa4ab2 ran `rm -rf /var/lib/mysql/*` to clear a stuck failed-join datadir; this was a reasonable corrective action (node2 held no synced cluster data yet) and the node subsequently joined cleanly via cmd-eca430f62445458f, but a destructive recursive rm is inherently higher-risk. A manual SST diagnostic on node1 (cmd-6ef8529def4c4467, sudo -u mysql mariadb-backup --target-dir=/tmp/backuptest) was not cleaned up — /tmp/backuptest is still present in the after-executor snapshot, an abandoned diagnostic artifact. Various small temp/log files (/tmp/install.log, /tmp/install.pid, /tmp/mysqld_sst.te, /tmp/mysqld_sst.mod, /tmp/reboot.log) also remain on all nodes post-task but are low-risk bounded residue. No failed/conflicting services, unsafe port exposure beyond the required Galera ports, or damaged package state were observed in the after-executor snapshot (failed-services empty on all nodes; package-health checks pass).","evidence_ids":["cmd-c044b37e74aa4ab2","cmd-eca430f62445458f","cmd-6ef8529def4c4467","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node3:failed-services"]},"overall_summary":"The executor built a working 3-node MariaDB Galera cluster on RHEL 9 (mariadb-server-galera 10.5.29 / galera 26.4.22), correctly diagnosed and fixed an SELinux AVC denial blocking the mariabackup SST socket connection, and directly demonstrated all material outcomes with observed state transitions rather than configuration alone: cross-node write replication, continued availability and write acceptance during a bounded single-node outage (2/3 quorum, Primary status), and automatic IST resynchronization on node3's return with the outage-period write intact. The independent full-cluster reboot evidence protocol was also executed cleanly for all three nodes — pre-restart boot_id/health/data baseline, reboot acknowledgement, distinct post-restart boot_id, and recovered Synced/Primary state with data intact — and is fully consistent with the fixed before-prepare/after-executor boot-identity snapshots. Hygiene is generally good; the main blemishes are a broad `rm -rf /var/lib/mysql/*` used to recover a stuck join on node2 (justified but risky) and an uncleaned diagnostic backup directory left on node1.","limitations":["Root/SST credentials were generated and distributed but not included in the report; the SST password did surface once in a debug `cat` command's captured tool output (cmd-6ef8529def4c4467 context), a minor secret-hygiene note rather than a functional gap.","No independent client-side (non-localhost) connectivity test was captured; all write/read verification used local `mysql` CLI on each node via /root/.my.cnf rather than a remote client connecting through the network, though this does not affect the cluster/replication verdict.","The diagnostic artifact /tmp/backuptest on node1 and the small residual temp files across all nodes were not cleaned up by the executor; this is noted in hygiene rather than affecting task functionality."]},"ttft_ms":30271,"type":"result","duration_ms":71833,"uuid":"00e4b450-be2d-4e9a-87d0-e1b84340a179","ttft_stream_ms":1335,"time_to_request_ms":56,"first_content_frame_ms":1644,"queued_turn_count":0}
